      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 20 mg is double the FDA-labeled maximum for adults, which is 10 mg once daily. Higher doses add little blood pressure benefit and raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia.\n\nPlease check whether 20 mg was intended. These are the usual options:\n\n- **Start at 5 mg once daily.** This is the typical starting dose. You can titrate to 10 mg after 1\u20132 weeks if needed. Use 2.5 mg for frail or elderly patients, or for patients with hepatic impairment.\n- **Order 10 mg once daily.** This is the maximum dose, for a patient who needs full dosing.\n- **Add a second agent.** If 10 mg isn't controlling BP, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is usually better than going above 10 mg.\n\nI'd suggest reviewing this with your resident or attending, and with pharmacy if you want a second check. If a supervising clinician confirms 20 mg is intentional, tell me and I'll enter it with a note documenting that. Or tell me the dose you want, such as 5 mg or 10 mg, and I'll enter it now."
      }
     ],
